{% extends 'emails/layout.html.twig' %}

{#
    The email that carries a purchase order to its vendor (#555).

    The `purchase_order_vendor` fallback view, in EmailNotifier's sense: PurchaseOrderController::
    send() asks EmailTemplateRenderer for the code first, and this renders only when nothing
    resolves — which today means "no admin has created a row for it". An admin who wants to reword
    it creates `purchase_order_vendor` in Settings → Email Templates and their row wins outright,
    exactly as it does for every other code in the catalogue.

    It ships as a view rather than as a 23rd entry in ShippedEmailTemplates because that class is
    byte-pinned to the migration chain by ShippedEmailTemplatesMatchTheChainTest — it is the record
    of the 22 templates #507 lifted OUT of the chain, and a 23rd would have to be seeded back INTO
    the chain to keep it green. See the note on the constants in PurchaseOrderController.

    Every value is read through |default() so that a copy of this body pasted into the admin
    template editor still previews: the preview renders against a fixed mock context that knows
    nothing about a purchase order, and a bare {{ purchase_order.x }} under strict_variables would
    fail there rather than render.

    Note what this does NOT say. It asks a supplier to fulfil an order; it does not ask anyone to
    pay anything, so there is no total-due language, no payment link and no terms-of-payment demand
    — the PO total is stated as what we expect to be charged, and the vendor's own invoice is what
    starts that conversation.
#}

{% block title %}Purchase order {{ purchase_order.poNumber|default('') }}{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <h2 style="margin-top: 0; color: #0f172a;">Purchase Order {{ purchase_order.poNumber|default('') }}</h2>
    <p>Hello <strong>{{ purchase_order.vendorName|default('') }}</strong>,</p>
    <p>
        Please find attached our purchase order
        <span class="highlight">{{ purchase_order.poNumber|default('') }}</span>.
        The PDF attached to this email is the order in full; the summary below is for convenience.
    </p>

    <div class="order-summary-box">
        <table class="detail-table">
            <tr>
                <td class="detail-label">PO number:</td>
                <td class="detail-value">{{ purchase_order.poNumber|default('') }}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="detail-label">PO date:</td>
                <td class="detail-value">{{ purchase_order.documentDate|default('-') }}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="detail-label">Requested delivery:</td>
                <td class="detail-value">{{ purchase_order.expectedDate|default('-') }}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="detail-label">Deliver to:</td>
                <td class="detail-value">{{ purchase_order.warehouse.name|default('-') }}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="detail-label">Payment terms:</td>
                <td class="detail-value">{{ purchase_order.paymentTerm|default('-') }}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="detail-label">Order total:</td>
                <td class="detail-value">{{ purchase_order.currency|default('') }} {{ purchase_order.total|default('0.00') }}</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>

    <table class="line-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Item</th>
                <th>Your part no.</th>
                <th>Quantity</th>
                <th>Unit cost</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for line in purchase_order.lines|default([]) %}
                <tr>
                    <td>{{ line.name }}</td>
                    <td>{{ line.vendorSku|default('-') }}</td>
                    <td>{{ line.quantityOrdered }}</td>
                    <td>{{ line.unitCost }}</td>
                </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
        Please confirm receipt of this order, along with your expected ship date, and quote
        {{ purchase_order.poNumber|default('this purchase order number') }} on your order
        confirmation, packing slips and invoice.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
